  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1146 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nElevated southerly winds will maintain a mild boundary layer\novernight with temperatures holding in the upper 50s to lower\n60s. Surface analysis shows an elongated cold front extending\nfrom Quebec through Eau Claire, WI into the Texas Panhandle, with\nweak low-pressure centers along the boundary and high pressure\nanchored over the eastern CONUS. Upper-level GOES imagery depicts\na longwave trough traversing the Plains, establishing\nsouthwesterly flow across the Mississippi Valley.\n\nThe trough advances east across the Mississippi Valley over the\nnext 24 hours, driving the cold front through the Mid-South.\nStrong poleward moisture transport will push surface dewpoints\ninto the upper 50s to near 60 F ahead of the front. Surface-based\nCAPE of 200-500 J/kg paired with ~35 kt bulk shear will move into\nsouthwestern portions of the CWA. Poor mid-level lapse rates\n(~5.5\u00c2\u00b0C/km) will limit convective organization, but isolated\nthunderstorms remain possible Sunday afternoon. Rainfall will be\nlight, averaging 0.25 inch or less with localized gaps in\ncoverage. The front exits east of the Tennessee River late Sunday\nnight, followed by post-frontal stratus.\n\nCool, dry air will build in behind the front through Tuesday,\nkeeping daytime highs in the lower 50s and overnight lows in the\n30s. An inverted trough moves westward into the region early\nWednesday, drawing moisture westward ahead of an approaching\nshortwave. Expect light rain showers to overspread the area from\nthe east, with maximum coverage concentrated along the Tennessee\nRiver Valley. Total rainfall will remain under 0.25 inch.\n\nAn active polar jet stream remains dominant across the CONUS\nwhile the subtropical jet stays displaced south, locking in a\ncool, dry pattern through the Thanksgiving holiday weekend.",
